  "designDoc": "# Short-link hardening design\n\n## Scope and compatibility\n\nThis is a brownfield change to the existing single-module Spring Boot service under `com.example.shortener`. The existing controller -> service -> repository layering, JPA entities, `ProblemDetail` exception handling, short-code generation, collision retries, analytics, and HTTP status behavior remain unchanged unless explicitly described below.\n\nThe human binding decisions take precedence:\n\n- Client identity is `HttpServletRequest.getRemoteAddr()`; forwarding headers are not trusted.\n- Rate limiting is in-process and per application instance.\n- Newly created links expire. Existing rows with a null expiration remain non-expiring for backward compatibility.\n- No private-network blocking or malicious-URL scanning is added.\n\nThe existing `spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=update` configuration adds the nullable expiration column in the current deployment model. Deployments managing schema outside Hibernate must add the equivalent nullable column to `links`.\n\n## Components and responsibilities\n\n### `LinkCreateRequest`\n\nExtend the existing record with `Integer expiresInDays`. Keep `url` validation unchanged and add optional Bean Validation constraints equivalent to `@Min(1)` and `@Max(365)`. The field must remain nullable so omission is valid. A JSON fractional number, string, or other non-integer value is rejected by Jackson binding and handled by the existing standard bad-request handler.\n\n### `Link`\n\nAdd a nullable `Instant expiresAt` mapped to a nullable database column such as `expires_at`. New constructors used by the service set this value; the protected JPA constructor and null handling preserve legacy rows. Provide a getter and retain all existing fields and behavior.\n\n### `PublicOriginProvider`\n\nContinue using the existing precedence: `app.public-origin` first, otherwise the current server configuration fallback. Resolve and validate one HTTP/HTTPS public origin at application startup. Expose a method returning the origin used for response construction, and retain `matches(URI)` for self-origin validation.\n\nFor comparison, canonicalize hostnames by:\n\n1. Converting the host to lowercase using `Locale.ROOT`.\n2. Removing all trailing dots.\n3. Converting the result with `IDN.toASCII(..., IDN.USE_STD3_ASCII_RULES)`.\n\nApply the same canonicalization to the configured origin and candidate destination. Compare scheme case-insensitively, canonical host, and effective port, where omitted HTTP and HTTPS ports are 80 and 443 respectively. Invalid canonicalization continues to result in the existing invalid-URL behavior. Response construction uses only the resolved configured/fallback origin and the short code; it never uses the request `Host` header.\n\n### `UrlValidator`\n\nRetain absolute HTTP/HTTPS, nonblank, and maximum-length validation. Call `PublicOriginProvider.matches` after parsing the URL and reject equivalent self-origin destinations with `InvalidUrlException`. Do not perform DNS resolution, private-network classification, reputation checks, or malicious-URL scanning.\n\n### `LinkCreationRateLimiter`\n\nAdd a singleton Spring component with a thread-safe map keyed by remote IP. Each value is a deque of admitted request timestamps. Admission atomically removes timestamps older than the rolling 60-second window, rejects when 30 timestamps remain, and otherwise appends the current timestamp.\n\nThe limiter is invoked only by `POST /api/links`, using `request.getRemoteAddr()`. `X-Forwarded-For`, `Forwarded`, and other headers are ignored. Empty buckets are removed when possible. Synchronization must make the check-and-add operation atomic for each IP, so concurrent requests cannot admit more than 30 requests in a window. The implementation is intentionally per JVM instance and does not add external infrastructure.\n\nA `RateLimitExceededException` is translated by the global handler into the same `ProblemDetail` format used by all other errors, with status 429.\n\n### `LinkService`\n\nKeep the existing ten-attempt short-code generation and unique-code race handling exactly as-is, including `CodeGenerationException` and HTTP 503 mapping.\n\nChange creation to accept the requested expiration duration, while retaining overloads needed by existing direct callers/tests if required. At creation:\n\n- Capture one `createdAt = Instant.now(clock)`.\n- Use `expiresInDays` when present, otherwise 90.\n- Compute `expiresAt = createdAt.plus((long) days, ChronoUnit.DAYS)`.\n- Persist the URL, generated code, zero click count, and expiration in the same `saveAndFlush` operation.\n\nThe injected `Clock` is the existing UTC clock bean, allowing fixed-clock tests.\n\nFor `follow`, load the link, preserve not-found behavior, then check expiration before creating a click event or incrementing the click count. A non-null `expiresAt` is expired when `!now.isBefore(expiresAt)`. Throw `LinkExpiredException` for an expired link. A null `expiresAt` means a legacy pre-change link and remains non-expiring. For a non-expired link, preserve the current referer normalization, click-event creation, counter increment, and destination return.\n\nThe expiration check and analytics writes occur in the existing transaction. The expired path performs no click-event insert and no click-count increment.\n\n### `LinkController`\n\nOn creation, apply the rate limiter using `getRemoteAddr()`, validate the request, call the service with URL and optional expiration, and build `shortUrl` from `PublicOriginProvider` plus the generated code. Return `201 Created` with the same response record and a `Location` header containing that configured origin URL.\n\nRemove request-derived scheme, server name, port, and context-path construction from the creation path. The request `Host` header must have no influence on either `shortUrl` or `Location`.\n\nExisting details and analytics endpoints remain available. Unless existing behavior explicitly requires changing them, details may retain their current response construction behavior; the configured-origin rule is mandatory for the link-creation response and its `Location` header.\n\n### `RedirectController`\n\nKeep the existing `302 Found` response and destination `Location` behavior for active links. Allow `LinkExpiredException` to propagate to the global handler, producing HTTP 410 and no redirect body.\n\n### `GlobalExceptionHandler`\n\nAdd handlers for `RateLimitExceededException` and `LinkExpiredException`, both using the existing private `problem(...)` helper so title, status, detail, and instance formatting remain compatible with current errors. Use status 429 and 410 respectively. Preserve all current handlers, including 400 validation, 404 not-found, 503 code-generation exhaustion, and 500 unexpected errors.\n\n## Request flows\n\n### Create link\n\n1. Spring binds JSON to `LinkCreateRequest` and performs Bean Validation.\n2. `LinkController` obtains `request.getRemoteAddr()` and asks `LinkCreationRateLimiter` to admit the request.\n3. `UrlValidator` parses and validates the destination and performs canonical self-origin comparison.\n4. `LinkService` calculates expiration using the UTC clock and defaults omitted duration to 90 days.\n5. The existing short-code generator attempts up to ten unique codes, preserving collision behavior and 503 exhaustion behavior.\n6. The link is persisted with expiration atomically.\n7. `PublicOriginProvider` builds the short URL from the configured/fallback origin and code.\n8. The controller returns 201, the existing response fields, and a `Location` header using that URL.\n\nValidation or rate-limit failures occur before persistence. Code-generation failure does not create a link and remains HTTP 503.\n\n### Redirect\n\n1. `RedirectController` calls `LinkService.follow` with the optional `Referer`.\n2. The service loads the link or raises the existing 404 exception.\n3. If `expiresAt` is non-null and the current instant is at or after it, raise `LinkExpiredException`.\n4. Otherwise record analytics and increment the click count using existing semantics.\n5. Return the stored destination and send the existing 302 redirect.\n\n### Details and analytics\n\nExisting detail lookup and analytics flows remain unchanged. Analytics remain readable for expired links; only redirect attempts after expiration are excluded from successful click analytics.\n\n## Data model\n\n`links` gains:\n\n| Column | Java type | Nullable | Meaning |\n|---|---|---:|---|\n| `expires_at` | `Instant` | yes | UTC expiration instant; null means a legacy non-expiring record |\n\nNew links always have a non-null `expires_at`, calculated from creation time. No backfill is performed for existing rows.\n\n## Error handling\n\nAll new errors use Spring `ProblemDetail` through `GlobalExceptionHandler`, preserving the existing standard body shape. Representative bodies contain the standard fields `type`, `title`, `status`, `detail`, and `instance` as produced by the existing helper.\n\n- Invalid or missing URL, invalid expiration range, fractional/string expiration, or malformed JSON: existing 400 behavior.\n- Self-origin destination: existing 400 invalid-URL behavior.\n- More than 30 admitted creation requests from one IP in the rolling 60-second window: 429.\n- Unknown code: existing 404 behavior.\n- Expired link: 410, with no redirect and no click recording.\n- Code-generation exhaustion: existing 503 behavior.\n- Other unexpected failures: existing 500 behavior.\n\n## Concurrency and reliability\n\n- The rate limiter's per-IP check and timestamp append are one atomic operation under per-bucket synchronization or equivalent concurrent coordination.\n- Stale timestamps are evicted on access, preventing unbounded timestamp growth for active clients. Empty buckets may be removed after eviction.\n- Concurrent link creation continues to rely on the database unique constraint and existing retry handling; no short-code algorithm changes are made.\n- Expiration is checked at redirect time using the injected clock. Equality with `expiresAt` is expired.\n- `Instant` values are persisted and compared in UTC. Hibernate's existing UTC JDBC configuration remains in use.\n- The in-memory limiter is deliberately not distributed. Multiple service instances can each admit up to 30 requests for the same IP; this is an accepted deployment limitation.\n- Clients behind a proxy are grouped by the direct connection address because forwarding headers are intentionally not trusted.\n\n## Testing requirements\n\nUse JUnit 5, AssertJ, Mockito, and `@SpringBootTest` with `@AutoConfigureMockMvc` for HTTP tests. Cover configured-origin output under forged `Host` headers, fallback-origin output, case/trailing-dot/Unicode/punycode self-origin rejection, acceptance of private destinations, 30/31 rate-limit boundaries, IP isolation, concurrent limiter access, omitted/1/365-day expiration, invalid expiration values, exact-boundary 410 behavior, null-expiration legacy links, analytics exclusion, active redirects, and preserved 503 exhaustion. Use fixed `Clock` instances in service tests.\n\n## Rejected alternatives\n\n- Trusting `Host`, `X-Forwarded-Host`, or `X-Forwarded-For` was rejected because it permits client-controlled origin construction or ambiguous client identity.\n- A distributed cache or database-backed limiter was rejected because the service must remain a small single service with no external infrastructure and the binding decision accepts per-instance enforcement.\n- A fixed-window limiter was rejected in favor of the required rolling one-minute window.\n- Retrofitting expiration onto legacy rows was rejected because the binding decision requires null-expiration records to remain non-expiring.\n- DNS/private-network blocking and malicious-URL scanning were rejected as explicitly out of scope.\n- Changing short-code generation or collision policy was rejected to preserve existing behavior, including HTTP 503 exhaustion.\n",
  "apiContract": [
